The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
const struct firmware *fw;
retval = request_firmware(&fw, sst->firmware_name, sst->dev);
if (fw == NULL) {
```
Reason:
The pointer `fw` is checked for `NULL` before verifying the return value of `request_firmware()`. If `request_firmware()` fails, `fw` remains uninitialized, leading to a potential use of an uninitialized variable when the code checks `fw == NULL`, which can cause undefined behavior or crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (retval) {
    dev_err(sst->dev, "request fw failed %d\n", retval);
    return retval;
}
if (fw == NULL) {
    dev_err(sst->dev, "fw is returning as null\n");
    return -EINVAL;
}
```
Reason:
The patch rearranges the checks so that the return value of `request_firmware()` is verified first. If `retval` indicates an error, it logs the error and returns immediately. This ensures that `fw` is only checked for `NULL` after confirming that the firmware request was successful, preventing the use of an uninitialized variable.

